{# ── Prefatory note ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
Preliminary Note
This document records the results of litigation research into the publicly visible Trustpilot review profile of National Homebuyers Limited (nationalhomebuyers.co.uk). It is produced for the purpose of internal case preparation and does not constitute expert evidence. The findings set out below are derived from a combination of direct observation of {{NHB}} Trustpilot profile and the peer-reviewed academic literature on online review manipulation. No expert witness has been commissioned at this stage; this document identifies the lines of academic authority that would support each indicator, for counsel's consideration.

The five indicators identified below are presented as research observations. Each is capable of independent corroboration. Their cumulative significance is addressed in the closing section.

{# ── Facts to be established ─────────────────────────────────────────── #}
Facts to be Established
{{ question_table( table_heading = "Core Questions Arising from {{NHB}} Trustpilot Profile", table_data = [ { "context": "Rating Distribution", "text": "Is {{NHB}} heavily polarised rating distribution (74% five-star, near-zero 2–4 star, 16% one-star) consistent with authentic organic reviewing activity, or does it indicate systematic manipulation of the review pool?" }, { "context": "Account History", "text": "Does the near-universal presence of single-review Trustpilot accounts among positive reviewers indicate coordinated inauthentic activity, and what is the significance of this under the academic literature on fake review detection?" }, { "context": "Transaction Stage", "text": "Why do a substantial number of five-star reviews describe only an initial telephone enquiry rather than a completed property transaction, and what does this indicate about {{NHB}} review solicitation practice?" }, { "context": "Linguistic Patterns", "text": "Does the repetitive naming of the same small group of {{NHB}} staff members across reviews authored by ostensibly independent reviewers indicate use of a review solicitation template or prompted campaign?" }, { "context": "Suppression Activity", "text": "What inference should be drawn from {{NHB}} submitting 30 review removal requests in a 12-month period, 25 of which were rejected by Trustpilot on their merits?" } ] ) }} {# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ INDICATOR 1 — J-CURVE / BIMODAL DISTRIBUTION ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Indicator 1 — Abnormal Rating Distribution
Observation
{{NHB}} Trustpilot rating distribution, as recorded in March 2026, is approximately: 74% five-star • 7% four-star • 2% three-star • 1% two-star • 16% one-star. The near-total absence of two-, three-, and four-star reviews produces a bimodal (J-shaped) distribution. In a service business spanning multi-week property transactions involving surveys, valuations, legal work, and completion, a distribution of this shape is not plausible as an organic outcome.

Supporting Academic Authority
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "HU-2006", quote_text = "In a controlled experiment where all respondents were required to submit reviews, the distribution normalised into an approximately bell-shaped curve. The J-shape observed in uncontrolled environments emerges from self-selection bias and, critically, from active manipulation of the review pool — not from genuine variation in service quality." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "N Hu, PA Pavlou and J Zhang", title = "Can Online Reviews Reveal a Product's True Quality? Empirical Findings and Analytical Modeling of Online Word-of-Mouth Communication", year = "2006", reporter = "Communications of the ACM", doc_type = "article" ) }}

Research Note
The Hu, Pavlou & Zhang finding is directly applicable here: {{NHB}} distribution is more extreme than the typical organic J-curve in that the middle ratings are almost entirely absent rather than merely under-represented. Under their framework, this is strongly indicative that reviewers with mixed or nuanced experiences are not reaching the platform — consistent with selective solicitation of only the most positively-disposed customers, or with systematic removal of middle-ground and negative reviews.

{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ INDICATOR 2 — SINGLE-REVIEW ACCOUNTS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Indicator 2 — Concentration of Single-Review Accounts
Observation
The overwhelming majority of recent five-star reviewers on {{NHB}} profile are listed as "GB • 1 review", indicating that the {{NHB}} review is the only review the account has ever posted on Trustpilot. Single-review accounts are structurally well-suited to coordinated inauthentic campaigns because the accounts can be created, used once, and abandoned without accumulating a history capable of cross-referencing.

Supporting Academic Authority
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "LZ-2016", quote_text = "Reviews flagged by Yelp's fraud detection algorithm as 'not recommended' are systematically more likely to originate from accounts with no prior review history. Reviewers with no social network connections or prior review history are more likely to be fake. Restaurants under competitive pressure show a significantly elevated rate of such accounts." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "M Luca and G Zervas", title = "Fake It Till You Make It: Reputation, Competition, and Yelp Review Fraud", year = "2016", reporter = "Management Science", volume = "62(12)", page = "3412", doc_type = "article" ) }}

Regulatory Authority
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "CMA-2021", quote_text = "An unusual number of reviews from first-time or single-review accounts is a primary indicator of coordinated inauthentic reviewing activity and is actionable under the Consumer Protection from Unfair Trading Regulations 2008." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "Competition and Markets Authority", title = "Online Reviews and Endorsements: Guidance for Businesses", year = "2021", reporter = "CMA123", doc_type = "article" ) }}

{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ INDICATOR 3 — PRE-TRANSACTION REVIEWS ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Indicator 3 — Reviews Solicited Prior to Transaction Completion
Observation
A significant number of {{NHB}} five-star reviews describe no more than an initial telephone enquiry or a single contact with a member of staff. {{NHB}} core transaction — acquiring a residential property — is a multi-week or multi-month process involving valuations, surveys, legal conveyancing, and formal completion. Reviews describing only an introductory call plainly pre-date any meaningful customer experience and cannot constitute a reliable measure of service quality for the transaction as a whole.

Supporting Academic Authority — Promotional Reviews Research
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "MDC-2014", quote_text = "Manipulated reviews disproportionately appear at early stages of a business relationship and lack the experiential specificity of genuine post-transaction reviews. Authentic reviews contain verifiable service-specific detail; promotional reviews are general, non-specific, and temporally displaced from the transaction." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "D Mayzlin, Y Dover and JA Chevalier", title = "Promotional Reviews: An Empirical Investigation of Online Review Manipulation", year = "2014", reporter = "American Economic Review", volume = "104(8)", page = "2421", doc_type = "article" ) }}

Supporting Academic Authority — Reality Monitoring
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "FP-2013", quote_text = "Deceptive reviews are characterised by high generality, absence of sensory or spatial detail, and absence of consequence-oriented language. Reviews describing only the beginning of an interaction — such as a telephone call prior to any service delivery — display the characteristics of deceptive rather than authentic reviews." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "T Fornaciari and M Poesio", title = "Automatic Deception Detection: Methods for Finding Fake Reviews", year = "2013", reporter = "Proceedings of the Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics", doc_type = "article" ) }}

{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ INDICATOR 4 — STAFF NAME-DROPPING ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Indicator 4 — Repetitive Staff Naming Consistent with Templated Solicitation
Observation
Multiple five-star reviews, authored by different and ostensibly unconnected reviewers, conspicuously name the same small group of {{NHB}} staff by first name (including, among others, Oliver, Jess, Guy, Elizabeth, Victoria, Callum, Meg, and Lisa). The probability that multiple independent reviewers would independently choose to name the same individuals without any prompt is very low. This pattern is structurally consistent with a review solicitation email or template that asks reviewers to name the individual they spoke with.

Supporting Academic Authority
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "LI-2014", quote_text = "Linguistic convergence across ostensibly independent reviews — including the naming of specific staff, recurrence of promotional language, and parallel narrative construction — constitutes evidence of a shared origin, whether a template, a paid campaign, or a solicitation prompt." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "H Li, B Liu, A Mukherjee and J Shao", title = "Spotting Fake Reviews using Positive-Unlabeled Learning", year = "2014", reporter = "Computación y Sistemas", volume = "18(3)", doc_type = "article" ) }}

Research Note — Trustpilot Removal Rules
It is noted that Trustpilot's own guidelines permit removal of reviews naming specific individuals in certain circumstances. This creates a potential asymmetry: a company may exploit the rule to flag and remove genuine negative reviews that name staff, while solicited positive reviews naming the same staff are not reported. This interaction between the staff-naming pattern and the removal activity documented in Indicator 5 below merits further investigation.

{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ INDICATOR 5 — REVIEW SUPPRESSION ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Indicator 5 — Systematic Suppression of Negative Reviews
Observation
{{NHB}} publicly visible Trustpilot profile records that the company submitted 30 review removal requests within a single 12-month period. Of those: Trustpilot rejected 25 on their merits; four were removed because Trustpilot could not obtain a response from the reviewer; and one was removed for breach of guidelines. The volume and rejection rate of these requests is inconsistent with good-faith use of the reporting mechanism and indicates a systematic campaign to suppress authentic negative reviews through attrition.

Comparative Regulatory Authority — FTC v NextMed
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "FTC-2025", quote_text = "NextMed systematically suppressed negative Trustpilot reviews by: (a) mass-flagging critical reviews to trigger automatic removal; and (b) offering financial incentives to consumers who would withdraw or amend negative reviews. These practices, in combination with positive review solicitation, constituted unfair or deceptive acts in breach of Section 5 of the FTC Act." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "Federal Trade Commission", title = "In the Matter of NextMed Inc", year = "2025", court = "FTC", doc_type = "case" ) }}

Note: While the FTC action is a US jurisdiction decision, it is cited here for the proposition that the conduct pattern — mass-flagging of negative reviews combined with positive review solicitation — has been recognised at regulatory level as a composite deceptive practice. The equivalent UK provisions are the Consumer Protection from Unfair Trading Regulations 2008, reg 5 and 6, and the Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2025 (fake reviews as an automatically unfair commercial practice under Schedule 1).

Supporting Investigative Data
{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "TP-TRANS", quote_text = "Businesses are approximately 33 times more likely to flag a negative review than a positive review. Approximately 60% of all flagged reviews are ultimately removed — because genuine customers frequently lack the documentation to defend their review against a commercial operator's challenge, and will not invest the time required to respond to Trustpilot's verification process." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "Trustpilot", title = "Transparency Report 2024–25", year = "2025", url = "https://transparency.trustpilot.com", doc_type = "article" ) }}

Prior Conduct — BBC Watchdog
National Homebuyers Limited was the subject of a BBC Watchdog investigation in the mid-2000s, in which the company was reported to have engaged in unscrupulous practices in its property acquisition business. This prior conduct is noted as background context relevant to the company's general disposition toward misleading commercial conduct. It is not relied upon as evidence of the current Trustpilot conduct but may be relevant to propensity arguments under the Criminal Justice Act 2003, ss 98–113, if and when an expert witness is commissioned to produce a formal report.

{# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ CUMULATIVE ASSESSMENT ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ #}
Cumulative Assessment of the Five Indicators
Each of the five indicators above is, taken individually, consistent with review manipulation. Their significance is considerably greater in combination. The peer-reviewed literature consistently demonstrates that individual signals may arise by chance but that their convergence is a reliable marker of systematic manipulation.

{{ text_quotation( quote_reference = "LZ-CLASS", quote_text = "The combination of (a) a J-shaped rating distribution, (b) single-review accounts, and (c) competitive pressure produces a reliable classifier for fake reviews, with a precision rate of 84% in the studied dataset." ) }}
{{ oscola_citation( authors = "M Luca and G Zervas", title = "Fake It Till You Make It: Reputation, Competition, and Yelp Review Fraud", year = "2016", reporter = "Management Science", volume = "62(12)", page = "3412", doc_type = "article" ) }}

All three factors in the Luca & Zervas classifier are present in {{NHB}} profile, to which two further indicators — templated staff-naming (Indicator 4) and the review suppression campaign (Indicator 5) — are added. The research position, at this preliminary stage and without expert evidence, is that the probability of all five indicators arising simultaneously in the absence of any manipulative activity is extremely low.

